        <body>
            <div xml:id="d329">

                <pb facs="0810" n="765" sameAs="frus:frus1977-80v19/pg765"/>

                <head>329. Memorandum From Secretary of State Vance to President Carter<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d329-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1979-03-15">March 15, 1979</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1. <hi rendition="#italic">Pakistan</hi>—Chris met with the Pakistan Ambassador
                    this afternoon and recapitulated his discussions in Islamabad with President Zia
                    and others.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d329-f2"/></p>
                <p>Chris made clear that Zia had not denied Pakistan’s nuclear intentions and
                    sensitive programs and that we consider this a virtual acknowledgement of such
                    activities. Chris reaffirmed our desire for a closer and more supportive
                    relationship with Pakistan, but noted that we must comply with the law
                    (Symington Amendment). A cut-off of aid would inevitably become public, whether
                    we wish it or not, and would have severe repercussions on our overall
                        relationship.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d329-f3"/></p>
                <p>The Pakistan Ambassador indicated he hoped that any damage to our relationship
                    could be limited. He affirmed that Pakistan’s with<pb facs="0811" n="766"
                        xml:id="pg766"/>drawal from CENTO in no way detracts from the importance
                    Pakistan attaches to its US ties, including the 1959 bilateral agreement.<anchor
                        n="4" xml:id="d329-f4"/></p>
                <p>[Omitted here is information unrelated to Pakistan.]</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d329-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d329-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Carter Library, Plains File, Box 39, State Department Evening Reports,
                    3/79. Secret. Carter wrote “Cy, J” in the upper right-hand corner of the
                    memorandum.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d329-f2" n="2" xml:id="d329-f2-content">
                <p>Telegram 65294 to Islamabad, March 16, relayed a full account of Christopher’s
                    meeting with Pakistan’s Ambassador, Sultan Mohammad Khan. (National Archives, RG
                    59, Central Foreign Policy File, P850027–2685)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d329-f3" n="3" xml:id="d329-f3-content">
                <p>In the left-hand margin next to the this paragraph, Carter wrote: “Cy—I would
                    hate to lose Pakistan or India. I’ll help as needed.”</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d329-f4" n="4" xml:id="d329-f4-content">
                <p>In telegram 3021 from Islamabad, March 13, the Embassy reported Shahnawaz’s
                    announcement that Pakistan—per formal procedures—intended to submit its
                    withdrawal notification from CENTO on August 22 and formally sever its links
                    with the organization in February 1980. (National Archives, RG 59, Central
                    Foreign Policy File, D790116–1167) For the Embassy’s analysis of Pakistan’s
                    withdrawal from CENTO, see <ref target="#d330">Document 330</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
